  <main class="container main-content">

    <!-- Short answer -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="answer-heading">
      <h2 id="answer-heading">Bangladeshi Divorce in the UAE: The Short Answer</h2>
      <p>
        You can get divorced in a UAE court, but the divorce only counts in Bangladesh once Bangladeshi
        procedure has been followed too. In the UAE, Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024, in force since
        15 April 2025, governs Muslim divorce for foreign residents unless one spouse insists on their own
        law (Article 1(3)). In Bangladesh, the Muslim Family Laws Ordinance 1961 applies to Muslim citizens
        &ldquo;wherever they may be&rdquo; (section 1(2)), and its section 7 decides when a talaq takes effect.
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>UAE side.</strong> A husband documents his talaq in court within 15 days (Article 58), or a wife files for khula or a judicial divorce.</li>
        <li><strong>Bangladesh side.</strong> Written notice to the Chairman, copy to the wife. The talaq takes effect 90 days after delivery, or when a pregnancy ends if later.</li>
        <li><strong>Registration.</strong> The Nikah Registrar (Kazi) registers the divorce, and the attested copy of that entry is the &ldquo;divorce paper&rdquo; used for passports and remarriage.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="stats-grid">
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-num">90 days</span>
          <span class="stat-label">after the Chairman receives the notice, a talaq takes effect in Bangladesh</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-num">15 days</span>
          <span class="stat-label">for a husband to document a talaq in a UAE court</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-num">Tk 10,000</span>
          <span class="stat-label">maximum fine for skipping the notice (jail up to 1 year is also possible)</span>
        </div>
        <div class="stat-card">
          <span class="stat-num">Tk 200</span>
          <span class="stat-label">court fee for a Family Court plaint in Bangladesh</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="info-box info-box--blue" style="margin-top:24px;">
        <h3>How this site earns money</h3>
        <p>
          DivorceU.AE is a referral service, not a law firm. We earn when readers ask to be put in touch
          with a UAE-licensed family lawyer. The Bangladeshi steps below are often done by the spouses
          themselves or with a lawyer in Bangladesh.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Which law -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="law-heading">
      <h2 id="law-heading">Which Law Applies: UAE Law, Bangladeshi Law, or Both?</h2>
      <p>
        Both, each in its own country: a UAE court applies UAE law, and Bangladeshi offices and courts
        apply Bangladeshi law when you take the papers home.
      </p>
      <h3>In the UAE</h3>
      <p>
        UAE courts hear personal status cases against foreigners who live or work in the UAE (Article 3).
        For a wife&rsquo;s claims for divorce, khula, dowry, alimony or custody, the competent court is
        where either spouse lives or works, or the marital home. Unless one of you insists on Bangladeshi
        law, the court applies the 2024 law.
      </p>
      <h3>In Bangladesh</h3>
      <p>
        The 1961 Ordinance and the Muslim Marriages and Divorces (Registration) Act 1974 both apply to
        Muslim citizens of Bangladesh wherever they may be (section 1(2) of each), so a Bangladeshi
        husband in Dubai is still bound by the section 7 notice rule. A foreign judgment is tested under
        section 13 of the Code of Civil Procedure 1908: it is conclusive unless it came from a court
        without jurisdiction, was not decided on the merits, refused to recognise Bangladeshi law where it
        applied, breached natural justice, was obtained by fraud, or upholds a claim founded on a breach
        of Bangladeshi law. The likely weak spots are a spouse who was never told about the case and a
        talaq never notified to the Chairman.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 7 -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="notice-heading">
      <h2 id="notice-heading">The Section 7 Talaq Notice: What Bangladeshi Law Requires</h2>
      <p>
        Section 7 of the Muslim Family Laws Ordinance 1961 requires a husband who pronounces talaq, in any
        form, to give the Chairman written notice as soon as may be and send a copy to his wife.
      </p>
      <div class="cost-table">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Section</th><th>What it says</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>7(1)</td><td>Written notice to the Chairman after talaq &ldquo;in any form whatsoever&rdquo;, copy to the wife.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>7(2)</td><td>No notice: simple imprisonment up to one year, a fine up to 10,000 taka, or both.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>7(3)</td><td>Unless revoked earlier, the talaq is not effective until 90 days after delivery to the Chairman.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>7(4)</td><td>Within 30 days, the Chairman forms an Arbitration Council to attempt reconciliation.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>7(5)</td><td>If the wife is pregnant, the talaq takes effect when the 90 days or the pregnancy end, whichever is later.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>7(6)</td><td>The couple may remarry each other without an intervening marriage, unless it was the third effective talaq.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="table-note">Source: Muslim Family Laws Ordinance 1961, official text on the Bangladesh laws portal.</p>
      </div>
      <h3>Who the &ldquo;Chairman&rdquo; is</h3>
      <p>
        Section 2(b) names the Chairman of the Union Parishad or Paurashava, or the Mayor or Administrator
        of a city corporation (the Ordinance still says Municipal Corporation); where a council has been
        superseded, the person appointed to run it. Rules under the Ordinance decide which area&rsquo;s
        office is competent, so confirm it with a Bangladeshi lawyer first: a notice sent to the wrong
        office is easy to dispute.
      </p>
      <h3>What a usable notice contains</h3>
      <p>
        The Ordinance only requires notice &ldquo;in writing&rdquo;. The PDFs that circulate online are not
        official forms. A notice that holds up usually states:
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Both spouses&rsquo; names, fathers&rsquo; names, NID or passport numbers and addresses, including your UAE address.</li>
        <li>The kabin nama details: date, Nikah Registrar, and register volume, page and serial number.</li>
        <li>The date of the talaq, your signature, and the Chairman&rsquo;s office as addressee, with the wife receiving a copy.</li>
        <li>Proof of delivery: registered post with acknowledgement due or tracked courier, because the 90 days run from delivery.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Section 8 applies the same procedure when a wife uses a delegated right of divorce
        (talaq-e-tafweez) or when either spouse ends the marriage otherwise than by talaq.
      </p>
      <div class="info-box info-box--gold">
        <h3>Power of attorney: check before you rely on it</h3>
        <p>
          The Bangladesh Consulate General in Dubai states on its power of attorney page that a power of
          attorney is not permissible in case of divorce. Plan on signing the notice yourself.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Scenario 1 -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="s1-heading">
      <h2 id="s1-heading">Scenario 1: Both Spouses Live in the UAE</h2>
      <p>
        If you both live in the UAE, a UAE court can divorce you, and you finish the Bangladeshi side
        afterwards by notice and registration.
      </p>
      <h3>The UAE step</h3>
      <p>
        A husband who pronounces talaq must document it before the competent court within 15 days
        (Article 58). If he does not, without an excuse the court accepts, his wife can claim compensation
        equal to alimony from the divorce to the documentation date, and she can file her own case to
        prove the divorce. Documentation is a duty with a penalty, not a condition of validity. A repeated
        talaq counts as one (Article 57), and a first or second talaq after consummation or valid seclusion is revocable
        during the iddah (Articles 59 to 61). More in our guide to
        <a href="/en/talaq-uae/">talaq in the UAE</a>.
      </p>
      <h3>The Bangladesh step</h3>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <h3>Attest the UAE papers</h3>
          <p>A certified copy of the divorce certificate or judgment, attested by the UAE Ministry of Foreign Affairs, then by the Bangladesh mission.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Send the notice</h3>
          <p>To the Chairman, copy to the wife, enclosing the attested UAE document. For a court judgment rather than a talaq, use a covering notice under section 8.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Let the 90 days run</h3>
          <p>The Chairman should form an Arbitration Council within 30 days. A talaq revoked before the 90 days end, for example by reconciling, never takes effect.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Register with the Nikah Registrar</h3>
          <p>Section 6 of the 1974 Act expects the person who effected the divorce to apply orally, so plan it for a visit home or ask a lawyer there. The attested copy of the entry is free (section 9).</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <h3>Does a UAE court divorce still need the Chairman notice?</h3>
      <p>
        For a talaq, yes: section 7 covers a talaq &ldquo;in any form whatsoever&rdquo;, and UAE
        documentation does not replace it. For a UAE judgment, such as khula or divorce for harm,
        Bangladeshi law has no written procedure for foreign decrees. Two provisions point the same way:
        section 8 applies the section 7 procedure to other dissolutions, and section 26 of the Family
        Courts Act 2023 makes a Bangladeshi Family Court send its divorce decree to the Chairman, with
        effect only 90 days after receipt. Bangladeshi practitioners advise the same for foreign decrees.
        No statute or reported decision we found settles it, so treat the notice as the safe course.
      </p>
      <h3>Worked example: two clocks running</h3>
      <p>
        An illustration, not a real case. A husband pronounces talaq in Sharjah on 1 March and documents
        it on 10 March. Under UAE law it is revocable during her iddah (Articles 59 and 84); if that ends
        in early June without a take-back, the marriage is over in UAE terms. The Union Parishad receives
        his section 7 notice on 15 May, so in Bangladesh the talaq is not effective until mid-August.
      </p>
      <p>
        In between, Bangladeshi law may still treat the marriage as subsisting. That matters if he marries
        again in Bangladesh: section 6 of the 1961 Ordinance requires the Arbitration Council&rsquo;s
        written permission for another marriage during an existing one, and a man who goes ahead without
        it must pay the whole dower at once. The fix is to send the notice the same week as the UAE
        documentation.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Scenario 2 -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="s2-heading">
      <h2 id="s2-heading">Scenario 2: Husband Works in the UAE, Wife Lives in Bangladesh</h2>
      <p>
        A husband in the UAE can divorce a wife in Bangladesh by sending the section 7 notice from here,
        and she can claim denmohor and maintenance in a Family Court near her home or in the UAE court
        where he lives.
      </p>
      <h3>Sending a talaq from the UAE</h3>
      <p>
        Sign the notice yourself, send it by registered post with acknowledgement due to the Chairman and
        to your wife, and keep the receipts. If you pronounced the talaq while living here, the 2024 law
        also expects you to document it in a UAE court within 15 days; where the respondent has no
        domicile in the UAE, the plaintiff&rsquo;s court is competent (Article 3(6)).
      </p>
      <h3>The wife&rsquo;s options in Bangladesh</h3>
      <p>
        The Family Courts Act 2023, which replaced the Family Courts Ordinance 1985, gives Family Courts
        jurisdiction over dissolution, dower, maintenance, and custody of children (section 5). For
        dissolution, dower and maintenance, the court where the wife ordinarily lives has jurisdiction
        (section 6), and the plaint fee is 200 taka (section 25). The court can also serve the husband by
        email if satisfied the address is correct (section 7). For maintenance during the marriage,
        section 9 of the 1961 Ordinance lets her apply to the Chairman, whose Arbitration Council can
        certify an amount. The catch is collection: a Bangladeshi decree does not enforce itself in the
        UAE.
      </p>
      <h3>The wife&rsquo;s options in the UAE</h3>
      <p>
        UAE courts hear a wife&rsquo;s dowry and alimony claims against a husband who lives or works here
        (Article 3). A plaintiff with no UAE domicile must name an elected domicile and a legal
        representative (Article 44(2)(a) of Federal Decree-Law No. 42 of 2022, the Civil Procedure Code),
        so she instructs a UAE lawyer, and the court can handle much of the case remotely (Articles 330
        and 331). The judgment is enforced in the UAE, where his salary and assets are.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Scenario 3 -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="s3-heading">
      <h2 id="s3-heading">Scenario 3: Wife in the UAE, Husband Left or Went Back to Bangladesh</h2>
      <p>
        A wife who still lives in the UAE can file for divorce here after her husband has gone, if he used
        to live or work here and then abandoned her and moved abroad, was deported, or has no known
        address abroad (Article 4(2)). She can also claim alimony as a UAE resident (Article 4(3)).
      </p>
      <p>
        The 2024 law has separate articles on divorce for failure to maintain (Article 77), desertion
        (Article 78) and absence, loss or imprisonment (Article 79); see our guide to
        <a href="/en/divorce-absent-husband-uae/">divorce when the husband is absent or abroad</a>. The
        Civil Procedure Code allows service by recorded call, SMS, smart applications or email, and failing
        that by posting on the court&rsquo;s website (Article 9(1)(a)).
      </p>
      <p>
        Keep proof of every attempt to reach him: section 13(d) of the Bangladeshi Code of Civil Procedure
        lets a court refuse a foreign judgment obtained in breach of natural justice, and &ldquo;he was
        never told&rdquo; is the obvious argument for a husband to raise. After the judgment, she sends
        the attested copy to the Chairman under section 8 and registers the divorce.
      </p>
      <p>
        She could instead sue in Bangladesh under the Dissolution of Muslim Marriages Act 1939, for
        example because his whereabouts have been unknown for four years or he has not maintained her for
        two years (section 2). A decree on the four-year ground waits six months, and his heirs must be
        notified (section 3). She can file where the couple last lived together (section 6 of the Family
        Courts Act 2023), normally through a lawyer there.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Scenario 4 -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="s4-heading">
      <h2 id="s4-heading">Scenario 4: The Wife Wants the Divorce</h2>
      <p>
        A Bangladeshi wife can end the marriage in the UAE through khula or a judicial divorce, or in
        Bangladesh through a Family Court suit under the 1939 Act or a delegated talaq written into her
        kabin nama.
      </p>
      <div class="two-col">
        <div class="info-box info-box--blue">
          <h3>In the UAE</h3>
          <p>
            Khula is a separation at the wife&rsquo;s request for a consideration, taking effect as a minor
            irrevocable divorce (Article 65). If the consideration is the dowry, returning what she
            received is enough; children&rsquo;s rights cannot be traded away; and if the husband
            stubbornly refuses, the court orders khula (Article 66). There is no iddah maintenance after
            khula (Article 101). See <a href="/en/khula-divorce-uae/">khula in the UAE</a>.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="info-box info-box--gold">
          <h3>In Bangladesh</h3>
          <p>
            Section 2 of the 1939 Act lists the grounds, including unknown whereabouts, no maintenance for
            two years, an additional wife taken in breach of the 1961 Ordinance, cruelty, and any other
            ground valid under Muslim law. The Family Court sends its decree to the Chairman within 7
            days, and it takes effect 90 days after receipt unless the couple reconcile (section 26,
            Family Courts Act 2023).
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <h3>If your kabin nama delegates the right of divorce</h3>
      <p>
        A kabin nama can record that the husband delegates the power of divorce to the wife
        (talaq-e-tafweez), which she exercises through the notice procedure (section 8 of the 1961
        Ordinance). The Nikah Registrar registers a tafweez divorce only on production of the registered
        delegation document or an attested copy of the register entry showing it (section 6(3) of the
        1974 Act), so get that copy first.
      </p>
      <h3>If the marriage was never consummated</h3>
      <p>
        Many couples register the nikah long before the wife joins the husband. With no intercourse and no
        valid seclusion, the UAE court must revoke the contract at the wife&rsquo;s request, without proof
        of harm, if the husband refuses to divorce or agree khula, she returns the dowry received, and
        reconciliation is impossible (Article 75). No iddah follows (Article 82).
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Denmohor -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="mahr-heading">
      <h2 id="mahr-heading">Denmohor: Claiming It in a UAE Court or in Bangladesh</h2>
      <p>
        You can claim denmohor, the dower written in the kabin nama, in either country: a UAE court can
        order it under Articles 45 to 47 of the 2024 law, and a Bangladeshi Family Court hears dower suits
        under section 5 of the Family Courts Act 2023.
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>UAE rule.</strong> Dowry may be deferred, but in all cases it becomes due on irrevocable separation or death (Article 46). A wife divorced before intercourse gets half of the specified dowry (Article 47(3)).</li>
        <li><strong>Bangladeshi rule.</strong> If the kabin nama gives no details of how the dower is paid, the whole amount is payable on demand (section 10 of the 1961 Ordinance). Dissolution under the 1939 Act does not affect her right to dower (section 5).</li>
        <li><strong>Proving it in the UAE.</strong> The court needs the kabin nama attested and translated into Arabic. The amount is in taka, so raise the dirham valuation with your lawyer early.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        An illustration: the kabin nama records Tk 5,00,000 of denmohor, Tk 1,00,000 of it paid at the
        wedding in jewellery. On an irrevocable divorce, the unpaid Tk 4,00,000 becomes due under Article
        46. A khula with the dower as the consideration (Article 66) would usually mean giving up that
        balance.
      </p>
      <p>
        A UAE court can also award iddah maintenance after a revocable divorce (Article 101) and, where the
        husband ends a consummated marriage by his sole will without her request or fault, compensation of
        up to one year&rsquo;s maintenance (Article 102). In Bangladesh, the Appellate Division held in
        Hefzur Rahman v Shamsun Nahar Begum that a divorced wife&rsquo;s maintenance is limited to the
        iddat period. Our <a href="/en/mahr-divorce-uae/">guide to mahr on divorce</a> covers prompt and
        deferred dower in more depth.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Children -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="children-heading">
      <h2 id="children-heading">Children: Custody, Passports and Travel Consent</h2>
      <p>
        In the UAE, custody goes first to the mother after separation (Article 114) until the child turns
        18 (Article 123), and at 15 the child may choose a parent (Article 122). In Bangladesh, the Family
        Court decides custody as the District Court under the Guardians and Wards Act 1890 (sections 5 and
        27 of the Family Courts Act 2023), guided by the child&rsquo;s welfare consistent with the
        child&rsquo;s personal law (section 17 of the 1890 Act).
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>Travel.</strong> The custodial parent needs the other parent&rsquo;s written approval to take the child abroad. Without it, a court can allow up to 60 days a year in total, with security for the child&rsquo;s return (Article 116).</li>
        <li><strong>Passports.</strong> The guardian, usually the father, may keep the child&rsquo;s passport except for travel, and the court can order it handed over if he refuses when it is needed (Article 117).</li>
        <li><strong>No Hague return remedy.</strong> The UAE is not a party to the 1980 Hague Child Abduction Convention, so that Convention cannot bring back a child taken out of the UAE.</li>
        <li><strong>Holidays in Bangladesh.</strong> Write dates, who holds the passport and the return date into the UAE settlement.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Comparison -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="compare-heading">
      <h2 id="compare-heading">UAE Law vs Bangladeshi Law at a Glance</h2>
      <p>
        The two systems agree on the basics of Muslim divorce but differ on notice, timing, custody and
        where to file.
      </p>
      <div class="cost-table">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Point</th><th>UAE (Decree-Law 41/2024)</th><th>Bangladesh</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Notice of talaq</td><td>Documented in court within 15 days (Art. 58)</td><td>Written notice to the Chairman, copy to the wife (MFLO s. 7(1))</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Waiting period</td><td>Iddah: three cycles, three lunar months, or until birth (Arts. 84, 85); for a court divorce, from the final judgment (Art. 83)</td><td>90 days from the Chairman&rsquo;s receipt, or end of pregnancy if later (s. 7(3), (5)); same 90 days for a court decree (2023 Act, s. 26)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Deferred dower</td><td>Due on irrevocable separation or death (Art. 46)</td><td>Whole dower payable on demand if the kabin nama is silent on payment (MFLO s. 10)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Custody</td><td>Mother first (Art. 114), until 18 (Art. 123), child chooses at 15 (Art. 122)</td><td>Family Court under the Guardians and Wards Act 1890; no single statutory age</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Remarrying each other</td><td>New contract and dowry after a minor irrevocable divorce (Art. 59)</td><td>No intervening marriage needed unless it was the third talaq (s. 7(6))</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Where to file</td><td>Where either spouse lives or works, or the marital home (Art. 3); wife in UAE whose husband left (Art. 4(2))</td><td>Where the cause arose or the couple last lived together; also where the wife lives (s. 6)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="table-note">A summary, not legal advice. MFLO = Muslim Family Laws Ordinance 1961.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Attestation -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="attest-heading">
      <h2 id="attest-heading">Attesting Divorce Papers Between the UAE and Bangladesh</h2>
      <p>
        Papers travel between the two countries by legalisation because the UAE is not a party to the
        Hague Apostille Convention. Bangladesh did accede on 29 July 2024, with effect
        from 30 March 2025, but an apostille only works between two member countries.
      </p>
      <h3>A UAE divorce document for use in Bangladesh</h3>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li>
          <h3>Court, then UAE MOFA</h3>
          <p>A certified copy from the court that documented the talaq or issued the judgment, attested by the UAE Ministry of Foreign Affairs.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Bangladesh mission</h3>
          <p>The Embassy in Abu Dhabi covers Abu Dhabi; the Consulate General in Dubai covers Dubai, Sharjah, Ajman, Fujairah, Ras Al Khaimah and Umm Al Quwain. Dubai lists foreign document attestation at AED 37 per page.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Translation</h3>
          <p>The judgment is in Arabic, so get an English translation from a legal translator, and ask the Bangladeshi office whether it also wants verification by the Ministry of Foreign Affairs in Dhaka.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <h3>A Bangladeshi document for use in the UAE</h3>
      <p>
        The kabin nama, divorce register entry or Family Court decree is typically attested in Dhaka by the
        Ministry of Law where required and the Ministry of Foreign Affairs, then by the UAE Embassy in
        Dhaka and the UAE Ministry of Foreign Affairs, then translated into Arabic. Fees change, so check
        before you go; the Abu Dhabi Embassy&rsquo;s notice of 19 May 2025 lists document attestation at
        AED 38 for the first five pages. More in our guide to
        <a href="/en/divorce-certificate-attestation-uae/">divorce certificate attestation</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Documents -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="docs-heading">
      <h2 id="docs-heading">Documents Checklist for Each Country</h2>
      <p>
        Most delays come from one missing attestation, so gather both sets first.
      </p>
      <h3>For the UAE court</h3>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Passports, Emirates IDs and residence details, or the last known UAE details of a spouse who left.</li>
        <li>Kabin nama and marriage certificate, attested in Bangladesh and the UAE, with an Arabic translation.</li>
        <li>Children&rsquo;s birth certificates; proof of income, rent, school fees and unpaid denmohor.</li>
        <li>The other spouse&rsquo;s phone, email and messaging details for service.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>For the Bangladesh steps</h3>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>The signed section 7 or 8 notice, with postal receipts and delivery confirmations.</li>
        <li>The attested UAE divorce certificate or judgment, with an English translation.</li>
        <li>Kabin nama copy with the register details, plus NID or passport copies of both spouses.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- After -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="after-heading">
      <h2 id="after-heading">After the Divorce: Remarriage, Passport and UAE Visa</h2>
      <p>
        Remarry only when both systems treat the divorce as final: in the UAE once the iddah has ended
        (Article 83), and in Bangladesh once the 90 days have run and the divorce is registered.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Remarriage.</strong> A Nikah Registrar registering a new marriage will normally want proof
        that the earlier one ended: the attested copy of the divorce entry.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Passport.</strong> The Bangladesh Embassy in Abu Dhabi asks e-passport applicants for a copy
        of the marriage or divorce registration certificate where applicable. Register the divorce in
        Bangladesh first, then update the passport.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>UAE visa.</strong> A wife sponsored by her husband has to move to her own sponsorship or
        another route. The rules for divorced women changed in 2025, so check current conditions with ICP or
        GDRFA, and read <a href="/en/visa-after-divorce-uae/">your UAE visa after divorce</a> before the
        divorce is final.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Non-Muslims -->
    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="nonmuslim-heading">
      <h2 id="nonmuslim-heading">Hindu and Christian Bangladeshis in the UAE</h2>
      <p>
        Non-Muslim Bangladeshis can use the UAE civil route under Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022, where
        either spouse can ask for a divorce without proving harm, but how Bangladesh treats that divorce is
        much less certain.
      </p>
      <p>
        The civil law applies to non-Muslim foreigners living in the UAE unless one spouse adheres to their
        home law (Article 1). Either spouse&rsquo;s wish to separate is enough (Article 7), the court
        decides after the other spouse is served (Article 8), and there is no Family Guidance (Article 3).
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Hindus.</strong> The Hindu Marriage Registration Act 2012 only covers registering
        marriages, which is optional; an unregistered Hindu marriage stays valid (section 3). It has no
        divorce provisions, and Bangladesh has no statute giving Hindus a general right to divorce.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Christians.</strong> Under section 2 of the Divorce Act 1869, a Bangladeshi court can
        dissolve a marriage only if a spouse is Christian and both are domiciled in Bangladesh when the
        petition is filed, and its grounds are narrow (section 10).
      </p>
      <p>
        If challenged in Bangladesh, a UAE no-fault divorce would face the section 13 test. We found no
        Bangladeshi decision on it, so take advice in both countries before remarrying there.
      </p>
    </section>

